        summary = "One nine-year common school for every child, paid for by the state with no fees. Parents choose among public schools under admission rules that stop selection by income, forbid tracking at about age ten, and exclude no pupil for any reason.",
        details = "Keeps: one common school for all pupils (Nordic social democracy, from Finland's comprehensive school reform (peruskoulu)); parental choice among competing schools (Classical laissez-faire capitalism, from Sweden's free-school reform); no fees for compulsory pupils (Chinese market socialism, from China's exemption from charges for rural compulsory education). Blocks: tracking at about age ten (Early tracking in the German school system), safeguarded by common lessons until the common school ends; selection by family income (Chile's school voucher reform), safeguarded by lotteries for oversubscribed places; exclusion of migrant pupils (China's exemption from charges for rural compulsory education), safeguarded by covering every pupil. Test: for about a decade, yearly national test results, the gap between low- and high-income pupils, a segregation index by municipality and the share of pupils on separate tracks, each compared with the year before the reform. Source: IFAU working paper (2006); SNS research brief 7; CESifo working paper 2007; New America, school choice in Chile; US-China Economic and Security Review Commission summaries; China Daily (2006).",
